## Using negative tests as a proxy for bias

Suppose the unmeasured confounder `U` pushes people to get tested when ill *regardless* of whether their illness is caused by the test-positive pathogen or something else as long as they produce similar symptoms. Then if symptoms are screened before a test is offered then we can plausibly assume `U` acts similarly on positive and negative hazards, i.e. 
$$
\beta_{1U} = \beta_{2U}.
$$
We call this **equi-confounding**. If we also assume that vaccine has no effect on any test-negative illnesses, then `T1` is a classic *negative outcome control* and the hazard ratio for testing-negative reveals the degree of residual confounding. We can then remove the bias from our original vaccine effectiveness estimate by taking the ratio of hazard ratios:

$$
\exp\{\beta_{2A}\} \approx \frac{\exp\{\beta^*_{2A}\}}{\exp\{\beta^*_{1A}\}},
$$

where $\beta^*_{2A}$ comes from the cause-specific model for testing positive and $\beta^*_{1A}$ from the model for testing negative, given by:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda_1(t | A, X) = \lambda_{10}(t) \exp(\beta^*_{1A}A + \beta^*_{1X} X) \\
\lambda_2(t | A, X) = \lambda_{20}(t) \exp(\beta^*_{2A}A + \beta^*_{2X} X)
\end{aligned}
$$

Applying this to refine our previous estimate, we find:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
fit_neg_once <- coxph(Surv(time, status == "Test negative") ~ A + X, data = example_df)
fit_pos_once <- coxph(Surv(time, status == "Test positive") ~ A + X, data = example_df)

tbl_merge(
  list(
    tbl_regression(fit_neg_once), 
    tbl_regression(fit_pos_once)
  ),
  tab_spanner = c("**Test negative**", "**Test positive**")
)
```

