The implied difference-in-differences estimate is the difference between the two vaccination coefficients^[Note: difference-in-differences on the log scale is the same as a ratio of hazard ratios on the multiplicative scale]. In this run we get roughly `-0.49`, closer to the truth than the naive `-0.24`.

Monte Carlo simulation confirms the unbiasedness of this approach when equi-confounding holds:



::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](index_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-8-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::



## Link to the classic test-negative design

If we subset to people who got tested and run a simple logistic regression of testing "positive vs negative" on vaccination, we recover the same value, `-0.49` as above. This is the familiar test-negative odds ratio.



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
tnd_fit_once <- glm(
  status == "Test positive" ~ A + X,
  data = subset(example_df, status != "Cens"),
  family = binomial(link = "logit")
)

tbl_regression(tnd_fit_once)
```

